# Mock F Paper 2: wrong answers + extra explanations for right ones (9, 11, 21). 'F: ' formula first, 'C: ' calculation step per line.
W={}
EXTRA={9,11,21}
def w(n,sel,trig,rule,expl): W[n]=dict(sel=sel,trig=trig,rule=rule,expl=expl)
w(4,["Finally, Stone reviews Brockway's fixed-income strategy"],["ten factors","several factors have limited historical data","The model is complex"],
 "Many parameters + little data = LARGE ESTIMATION ERROR (the model is detailed, but its parameters cannot be estimated precisely). Too simple a model = high specification error.",
 ["Ten factors, many parameters, several factors with limited history → the parameters cannot be estimated precisely → large estimation error (A).",
  "Specification error (option B) is high when the model is too simplistic. This model is complex, so it is low.",
  "Your pick C: a Student's t-distribution has FAT tails, so it gives a HIGH probability of extreme returns, not a low one."])
w(5,["Chun Tian is a US-based portfolio manager","Three months ago, Tian made a 6-month deposit","@Exhibit 1"],["made a 6-month deposit of 50 million Australian dollars (AUD)","fully hedged the deposit","reference rates have not changed over the past three months","USD reference rate (annualized) | 0.26% | 0.36%"],
 "Covered interest rate parity: a fully hedged foreign deposit earns the DOMESTIC (USD) rate for the SAME TERM as the deposit. The deposit term is 6 months, so use the 6-month USD rate.",
 ["F: Hedged all-in return on the foreign deposit = domestic reference rate of the same term (covered interest rate parity)",
  "The deposit has a 6-month term, so the matching rate is the 6-month USD reference rate: 0.36% (B).",
  "Your pick A (0.26%) is the 3-MONTH USD rate: it comes from the 3 months that have passed since the deposit started, not from the deposit term.",
  "Option C (0.67%) is the 6-month AUD rate: the question asks for the return in USD terms."])
w(9,["Fabiano Arlandi","Company A is a high-quality issuer","Company B was recently acquired","Company C operates"],["high-quality issuer with a strong competitive position in a stable industry","currently refinancing near-term maturities with long-term debt","recently acquired in a leveraged buyout","at the bottom of its economic cycle"],
 "Upward-sloping credit curve: strong, stable issuer (low short-term spread that rises with maturity). Inverted curve: leveraged buyout (investors expect improvement) or a cyclical firm at the bottom of its cycle.",
 ["Company A: high quality, stable industry, refinancing short-term debt with long-term debt → very low short-term spreads rising with maturity → upward sloping (A).",
  "Company B (leveraged buyout): inverted, as investors expect restructuring to improve cash flows.",
  "Company C (bottom of the cycle): inverted, as investors expect recovery."])
w(11,["Three months ago, Arlandi bought $10 million of 5-year protection"],["bought $10 million of 5-year protection","narrowed from 350 bps to 220 bps","contract with a duration of 3.7 years"],
 "CDS value change ≈ −(Δspread) × duration × notional for the PROTECTION BUYER. Spreads narrowed → buyer loses. Use duration, not maturity, and the CHANGE in spread.",
 ["F: Loss ≈ Change in credit spread × Duration × Notional",
  "C: Change in spread = 220 − 350 = −130 bps = −1.30%",
  "C: −1.30% × 3.7 × $10,000,000 = −$481,000 (A)",
  "Option B ($650,000) uses maturity 5 instead of duration 3.7.",
  "Option C ($814,000) uses the ending spread of 220 bps instead of the change."])
w(15,["There are rumors that Boston Terminals plans to acquire a competitor","FCFF is estimated at","WACC is 12%","Market value of non-operating assets","Book value of debt is"],["FCFF is estimated at $368 million in Year 1, $442 million in Year 2 and is expected to grow by 3% per year afterwards","whose shares are currently trading at $24.1 per share","Market value of non-operating assets is $300 million","market value of debt is $2,300 million"],
 "Two-stage FCFF: discount FCFF at WACC and add a terminal value at the end of Year 2. Equity = firm value + non-operating assets − MARKET value of debt. Then compare per-share value with the price: fair value above price = undervalued.",
 ["F: Firm value = FCFF1/(1+WACC) + (FCFF2 + TV2)/(1+WACC)^2",
  "F: TV2 = FCFF3/(WACC − g), with FCFF3 = FCFF2 × (1 + g)",
  "F: Equity value = Firm value + Non-operating assets − Market value of debt",
  "C: FCFF3 = 442 × 1.03 = 455.3",
  "C: TV2 = 455.3 / (0.12 − 0.03) = 5,058.4",
  "C: PV of Year 1 = 368 / 1.12 = 328.6",
  "C: PV of Year 2 and TV2 = (442 + 5,058.4) / 1.12^2 = 4,384.9",
  "C: Firm value = 328.6 + 4,384.9 = 4,713.5 (the portal shows 4,709.5 with different rounding)",
  "C: Equity = 4,713.5 + 300 − 2,300 = 2,713.5",
  "C: Per share = 2,713.5 / 100 = $27.1",
  "Fair value $27.1 > price $24.1 → undervalued (A).",
  "Your pick C (overvalued) reverses the comparison: a fair value above the market price means the stock is cheap.",
  "Option B ($24.1) forgets the non-operating assets: (4,713.5 − 2,300) / 100."])
w(16,["Bernal thinks that the competitor is likely to deploy anti-takeover measures"],["the competitor will issue new debt and use the proceeds to buy-back its own shares"],
 "FCFF is the cash flow BEFORE financing, so new debt and buybacks do not change it. FCFE includes net borrowing, so new debt raises FCFE in the year of issuance.",
 ["F: FCFE = FCFF − Interest × (1 − t) + Net borrowing",
  "Debt issuance and buybacks are financing flows. FCFF is before financing → no impact.",
  "New borrowing raises net borrowing → FCFE increases in the year of issuance (B).",
  "The share buyback is a USE of FCFE (payment to equity holders), not a change in it.",
  "Your pick A (FCFF up, FCFE down) has both effects wrong."])
w(18,["In a recently published investment report, Li included a table"],["Li did not cite the source of the table in her report nor did she verify the statistical analysis","well-recognized industry journal"],
 "Standard I(C) Misrepresentation: using someone else's material without citing the source is plagiarism. Not verifying a table from a reputable source is NOT a violation (V(A) lets her rely on it).",
 ["Table copied from an industry journal, no citation → implies it is her own work → plagiarism, violation of I(C) (B).",
  "Not verifying is acceptable here: the source is a well-recognized journal, so she may rely on it under V(A).",
  "Your pick A (no violation) missed the missing citation."])
